# Recorded from a second runtime; see docs/reference-runtime.md. The model is
# NOT committed -- see docs/fixture-provenance.md. No mandatory test reads
# this file.
#
# The vector below is what llama.cpp produced for this prompt, pooled and at
# unit length, recorded whole as the other embedding recordings are.
#
# What this one is for is the code variant of jina-bert-v2: the one
# published file of the architecture that carries a centred normalization
# over the whole of the queries and another over the whole of the keys,
# and a second normalization of the attention sublayer over the joined
# residual with the layer's input added once more -- six tensors a block
# the text variant has not got, which this build refused by name until it
# computed them. The fixture crosses the arithmetic with the independent
# implementation; what a fixture cannot say is that the arrangement is the
# one the model was trained with, and this is what says it.
#
# Two other things this file decides. Its vocabulary is a byte-pair one
# under a bert arrangement -- tokenizer.ggml.pre jina-v2-code, sixty-one
# thousand pieces, the text framed by <s> and </s> as the file's cls and
# separator -- where every other bert here is WordPiece. And its epsilon is
# a millionth of a millionth, which the file states and a reader that
# defaulted the bert epsilon would not.
#
# The weights are F16 for the reason the other embedding recordings are:
# against a quantized file the two runtimes differ by the other one's
# activation quantization rather than by anything here.
#
# Taken with
#   llama-embedding -m jina-v2-base-code.f16.gguf -p "def add(a, b): return a + b"
#     --pooling mean --embd-normalize 2 --embd-output-format json --device none
# The tokens are [0, 406, 1039, 12, 69, 16, 328, 872, 437, 323, 464, 328, 2]
# on both runtimes.

runtime llama.cpp 95b8e33
model jina-v2-base-code.f16.gguf
prompt def add(a, b): return a + b
pooling mean
tolerance 5.0E-04

